Id = “searchBox” , #id —> css for that element
class= “classname” , .classname —> for that element
placeholder= “enter name” , [placeholder=‘value’]
.classname[attribute=‘value’]


All locators in playwright by default work with elements in shadow DOM


Hard assertion

await expect(locator).toHaveText('Welcome');
await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
await expect(locator).not.toBeVisible();   // negated

The test stops at the first failure.

Soft assertion

await expect.soft(locator).toHaveText('Welcome');
await expect.soft(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
await expect.soft(locator).not.toBeVisible();   // negated

The test continues after a failure and is marked failed at the end.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

WebKit is the browser engine that powers Apple's Safari. It's the part of the browser that renders web pages
(HTML, CSS, JavaScript).

In your line, addopts = --headed --browser webkit looks like a pytest config (probably pytest-playwright):

- addopts makes pytest add these options to every run automatically.
- --headed runs the browser with a visible window. The default is headless, with no window.
- --browser webkit runs your tests in Playwright's WebKit build. That's a close stand-in for Safari. The other choices
    are chromium (Chrome/Edge) and firefox.

Why it matters for QA: Safari renders some things differently from Chrome. If your screenshot doesn't match what you
expected, the browser could be the cause. A baseline screenshot taken in Chromium will often differ from one taken
in WebKit because of font rendering, spacing, and similar details.

Playwright's WebKit isn't the real Safari app. It's close, but not identical. It also runs on Windows and Linux,
which ties back to your earlier "updates for Windows" question.

