In Python, a directory is any plain folder. A package is a folder that Python can import code from.

Directory

- Any normal folder, such as screenshots/, reports/, test_data/ or videos/.
- It usually holds non-code files: JSON, CSV, images, logs, reports.
- You can't reliably import from it.

Package

- A folder that contains an __init__.py file, which can be empty.
- It holds Python modules (.py files) that you import elsewhere.
- Example: from pages.login_page import LoginPage.
- In PyCharm, "New → Python Package" creates the folder and __init__.py together. "New → Directory" creates only 
the folder.

Typical Playwright project layout

pw_project/
├── pages/              # PACKAGE (has __init__.py) – Page Object classes
│   ├── __init__.py
│   └── login_page.py
├── tests/              # PACKAGE – test files
│   ├── __init__.py
│   └── test_login.py
├── utils/              # PACKAGE – helpers
│   ├── __init__.py
│   └── helpers.py
├── test_data/          # DIRECTORY – users.json, data.csv
├── reports/            # DIRECTORY – HTML reports
├── screenshots/        # DIRECTORY – failure screenshots
├── conftest.py
└── pytest.ini

Rule of thumb

- If it holds Python code you'll import, make it a package.
- If it holds data or output files, make it a directory.

Since Python 3.3, folders without __init__.py can still be imported as "namespace packages". Pytest and tooling 
can behave unpredictably with them, though. Adding __init__.py is the safer habit, especially in tests/, 
where it avoids duplicate-filename conflicts.

